WEBVTT

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:07.146
My laptop computed the same picture four ways: a CPU core with plain code, a CPU

00:00:07.146 --> 00:00:12.359
core with instructions that handle 8 numbers at once, the graphics card with a

00:00:12.359 --> 00:00:15.901
single thread, and the graphics card with 32 threads.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:17.018
Pause.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:20.314
Write down which finished first, and by how many times.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.089
A number, not a feeling.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.609
A processor executes instructions: add these two numbers, multiply those, compare,

00:00:35.609 --> 00:00:35.936
jump.

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:41.873
The CPU, the central processor, is built to finish a single sequence of instructions

00:00:41.873 --> 00:00:43.202
as fast as possible.

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:48.883
The GPU, the graphics processor, has far more arithmetic circuits, each one simpler,

00:00:48.883 --> 00:00:54.089
and it is fast only when many of them get the same instruction at the same moment.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:59.439
That difference is why GPUs now carry most of the world's AI arithmetic, and why

00:00:59.439 --> 00:01:00.627
they are expensive.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:05.460
A GPU that is not kept busy is money spent on circuits doing nothing.

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:10.577
Underneath every detail, this series measures how much of the hardware did useful

00:01:10.577 --> 00:01:12.402
work, and why the rest did not.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:22.963
The CPU is a Ryzen 5 4600H.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:28.457
It has 6 cores; a core is a part of the CPU that executes its own sequence of

00:01:28.457 --> 00:01:29.326
instructions.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:35.227
Each core has AVX2 instructions, which act on a 256-bit register.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:39.858
A float, a fraction stored in 32 bits, takes 32 of those bits.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:40.978
Pause.

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:46.717
Calculate how many floats one AVX2 instruction handles, and how many the 6 cores

00:01:46.717 --> 00:01:47.794
handle together.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:58.769
Dividing 256 by 32 gives 8 floats per instruction.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.195
Across the 6 cores that makes 48 float operations at once.

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:06.345
The GPU is a GTX 1650.

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:12.188
NVIDIA builds the chip from identical blocks of hardware called SMs, and this one

00:02:12.188 --> 00:02:12.663
has 14.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:16.199
Each SM has 64 FP32 units.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:22.705
An FP32 unit is a circuit that does a 32-bit multiply-add for a single thread.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:23.828
Pause.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:27.849
Calculate the total, and compare it with the CPU's 48.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:44.419
The chip has 14 × 64 = 896 FP32 units, and NVIDIA's own listing says 896 CUDA cores.

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:49.655
On paper that is 18.7 times more float circuits than the CPU.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:56.486
These are measured times from Lab 1 of MIT course 6.S894, Accelerated Computing, on

00:02:56.486 --> 00:02:57.424
this laptop.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:58.548
Pause.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:02.218
Divide the plain CPU time by each of the other three.

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:12.175
AVX2 is 7.07 times faster.

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:20.517
The single GPU thread gives 0.153, which means it is 6.55 times slower than a plain

00:03:20.517 --> 00:03:21.383
CPU core.

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:26.082
The 32 GPU threads are 3.47 times faster.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:32.105
The fastest of the four is the CPU with AVX2, at 8.71 milliseconds.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:35.745
The AVX2 time used a single core out of 6.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:44.444
The 32-thread GPU time used 32 threads, so at most 32 of the 896 FP32 units at any

00:03:44.444 --> 00:03:45.157
moment.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:46.278
Pause.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:47.953
Calculate both shares.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:58.268
The CPU result used 16.7 percent of its cores.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:03.505
The GPU result used 3.6 percent of its FP32 units.

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:07.250
Put the numbers side by side.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:14.235
The CPU beat the GPU, 8.71 against 17.732 milliseconds.

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:18.835
Someone concludes that the GPU is slower than the CPU at this job.

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:19.948
Pause.

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:24.268
Which single number on the screen says the comparison does not support that?

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:32.711
The answer is 3.6 percent.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:38.680
The GPU result used about a twenty-eighth of its arithmetic circuits; the CPU result

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:40.420
used a sixth of its cores.

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:51.132
If every FP32 unit had done as much as each of the 32 did, the time would be 17.732

00:04:51.132 --> 00:04:55.127
× 32 / 896 = 0.633 milliseconds.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:05:00.155
That is a straight-line estimate that ignores everything except the count of units,

00:05:00.155 --> 00:05:01.112
not a prediction.

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:07.540
Its only job is to show how much hardware the 17.732 left unused.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:13.915
The tempting answer, "GPUs are slower than CPUs for this job", fails on the 3.6

00:05:13.915 --> 00:05:14.525
percent.

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:18.825
A GPU is not fast by itself.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:23.636
It is fast when a program gives most of its circuits the same instruction at the

00:05:23.636 --> 00:05:24.193
same time.

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:30.252
The first GPU version here used a single thread and lost to a CPU core by more than

00:05:30.252 --> 00:05:30.940
six times.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:37.178
The second used 32 threads and still left over 96 percent of the arithmetic circuits

00:05:37.178 --> 00:05:37.669
unused.

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:42.520
For a manager: the hardware you buy and the hardware your software uses are two

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:43.545
different numbers.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:45.095
Ask for the second.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:56.953
The CPU with AVX2 finished first, about 2.04 times sooner than the 32 GPU threads:

00:05:56.953 --> 00:05:59.677
17.732 divided by 8.71.

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:02.782
Count your guess as close if it was within a fifth of that.

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:05.626
Keep a tally on paper, a line per video.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:09.871
Suppose you could change only the launch.

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:15.388
How would you find out whether this GPU can beat 8.71 milliseconds, and which two

00:06:15.388 --> 00:06:16.919
numbers would you watch?

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:18.028
Pause.

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:30.037
Launch enough blocks to give all 14 SMs work, each block a multiple of 32 threads,

00:06:30.037 --> 00:06:30.865
and time it.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:32.244
Watch the time.

00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:41.044
Also watch the total of all 65,536 results, which my program prints as 16,274,722

00:06:41.044 --> 00:06:48.142
when the picture is right; if that total changes, the speed is fake.

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:53.610
And do not divide 17.732 by 28 to predict the answer.

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:58.419
The places of this picture need very different amounts of arithmetic, so measure it.

